{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And when an ox gores a man.</b> With reference to a man it uses the language of goring (נגיחה), but with reference to a beast, an ox that killed an ox, it uses the language of נגיפה and says ״וכי יגוף״. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said in Bava Kamma: a man has mazal, so it used of him the language of נגיחה — that is, he did it intentionally and with particular heedfulness; a beast has no mazal, so it used of it the language of נגיפה — that is, by way of accident." "<b>The ox shall be surely stoned.</b> The death of the ox is not to exact justice from the ox, but to exact justice from its owner, so that he will be careful to guard it; and if he is not careful, he knows that he will lose his money. This is by way of the plain sense (peshat), and it is the opinion of the Ramban." "<b>However,</b> there is another opinion: an ox that killed a man — and likewise a wild beast or a bird that killed, under any circumstances — has bound to it the force that was bound to the primordial serpent. Therefore the Sages compared the plague of pestilence to the goring of the ox, from what they said in tractate Ta'anit at the beginning of the chapter Seder Ta'aniyyot: three dead in one day — this is nothing; in three days, one after another — this is pestilence. And likewise with an ox: if it gored three times in one day, this is not warned (מועד); three times in three days, one after another — this is warned, as it is said: ״ואם שור נגח הוא מתמול שלשום״ — the words of R. Yehudah. And R. Meir disagrees with him, for he says: if its gorings were far apart, he is liable; if its gorings were close together, no need to say. Even so, the halakhah follows R. Yehudah. And they further said that an ox in the days of Nisan — the Satan dances between its horns, and it is known that it draws force from the attribute of Judgment, as it is written (Ezekiel 1:10): ״ופני שור מהשמאל״. And the Sages enacted a measure with a rooster that killed a person: they brought it to a court and stoned it, and the verse speaks of the common case when it mentions ox, but the same applies to the rest of living creatures." "<b>And you already know</b> that in the time of the Talmud the Satan would disguise himself and appear to the eyes of creatures, until they needed to seek mercy that he be hidden from them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {2 "The phrase דאמר introduces the baraita in Ta'anit; the text appears to have lost a word (typically שלשה מתים) before מתים ביום אחד. Translated hyper-literally as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:49.973864679Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}